e can express \\(a_1\\) from the first equation:\n\\[ a_1 = \\frac{1053}{r^2} \\]\n\nSubstitute this expression for \\(a_1\\) into the second equation:\n\\[ \\frac{1053}{r^2} \\cdot r^8 = \\frac{13}{9} \\]\n\\[ 1053 \\cdot r^6 = \\frac{13}{9} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\frac{13}{9 \\cdot 1053} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\frac{13}{9477} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\frac{13}{13 \\cdot 729} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\frac{1}{729} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6 \\]\n\\[ r = \\frac{1}{3} \\text{ or } r = -\\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(r = \\frac{1}{3}\\) (or \\(r = -\\frac{1}{3}\\)) back into the expression for \\(a_1\\):\n\\[ a_1 = \\frac{1053}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2} = \\frac{1053}{\\frac{1}{9}} = 1053 \\cdot 9 = 9477 \\]\n\nWe need to find the seventh term \\(a_7\\):\n\\[ a_7 = a_1 \\cdot r^6 = 9477 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6 = 9477 \\cdot \\frac{1}{729} = 13 \\]\n\nThus, the seventh term is:\n\\[ \\boxed{13} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve this problem, we need to use the general formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of a geometric sequence, which is given by:\n\\[ a_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1} \\]\nwhere \\(a_1\\) is the first term and \\(r\\) is the common ratio.\n\nGiven that the third term \\(a_3 = 1053\\) and the ninth term \\(a_9 = \\frac{13}{9}\\), we can write these terms using the general formula:\n\\[ a_3 = a_1 \\cdot r^2 = 1053 \\]\n\\[ a_9 = a_1 \\cdot r^8 = \\frac{13}{9} \\]\n\nWe can express \\(a_1\\) from the first equation:\n\\[ a_1 = \\frac{1053}{r^2} \\]\n\nSubstitute this expression for \\(a_1\\) into the second equation:\n\\[ \\frac{1053}{r^2} \\cdot r^8 = \\frac{13}{9} \\]\n\\[ 1053 \\cdot r^6 = \\frac{13}{9} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\frac{13}{9 \\cdot 1053} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\frac{13}{9477} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\frac{13}{13 \\cdot 729} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\frac{1}{729} \\]\n\\[ r^6 = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6 \\]\n\\[ r = \\frac{1}{3} \\text{ or } r = -\\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(r = \\frac{1}{3}\\) (or \\(r = -\\frac{1}{3}\\)) back into the expression for \\(a_1\\):\n\\[ a_1 = \\frac{1053}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2} = \\frac{1053}{\\frac{1}{9}} = 1053 \\cdot 9 = 9477 \\]\n\nWe need to find the seventh term \\(a_7\\):\n\\[ a_7 = a_1 \\cdot r^6 = 9477 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6 = 9477 \\cdot \\frac{1}{729} = 13 \\]\n\nThus, the seventh term is:\n\\[ \\boxed{13} \\]","id":"algebra:753"}
